---
title: FrontierJS Supply Co. — Built for The Wild
description: Gear for developers who get their hands dirty. Tees, hoodies, caps and mugs for the three realms.
render: static
---
<!--
  The front page, and the storefront's one piece of marketing.

  Prerendered — every byte below is in the file a visitor gets, artwork and
  merch grid alike, and there is no JavaScript on this page at all. That is not
  a nice-to-have here: `verify:site` reads the built HTML and asserts the page
  made ZERO calls to the API and carries exactly one <h1>. A promo page whose
  product names arrive from a fetch is a page a crawler sees empty, and it looks
  identical in a browser.

  So the merch grid is baked by `index.meta.js` at build time, and the artwork
  is served from `public/brand/` as ordinary files — neither is an API call.

  ── The dark sections ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  `theme-dark` on a section, not a hand-mixed palette. A theme in
  @frontierjs/css is a class of inheriting tokens and nothing else, so putting
  one on an element hands every descendant the whole inverted ramp — ink, rules,
  surfaces, AND `--tone-l-min`/`--tone-l-max`, the window a tone is clamped into
  when it is rendered as text. That last pair is the one a hand-rolled dark
  section always forgets: tones are tuned as fills behind white text and mostly
  fail as text on a dark ground, and nothing in CSS can derive the correction
  because relative color syntax cannot read the surface a color will land on.

  What this file then overrides is the three surface COLORS, to the brand's
  Dusk Navy. Every derivation on top of them stays the package's.

  ── The brand ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  The palette, the wordmark voice and the artwork all come from the brand
  sheets in `db/brand-sheets/`, which is also where the shop's own product
  photography was cut from. The three archetypes and the trail panels are
  crops of those sheets; `db/brand-sheets/README.md` says which region each one
  is, so a re-cut is repeatable rather than a file somebody once made.
-->
<script>
  import { priceRange } from '../money.js'

  export let data = null

  const products = data?.products ?? []

  // The brand's own three archetypes, with its own words. Data rather than
  // markup because the three are parallel and a copy edit should be one line.
  const ARCHETYPES = [
    { id: 'explorer', name: 'The Explorer', art: '/brand/arch-explorer.png',
      line: 'Walks first. Finds what matters. Defines the path forward.' },
    { id: 'hawk',     name: 'The Hawk',     art: '/brand/arch-hawk.png',
      line: 'Sees the whole territory. Maps the relationships. Ensures nothing is missed.' },
    { id: 'wolf',     name: 'The Wolf',     art: '/brand/arch-wolf.png',
      line: 'Executes with precision. Protects the system as it moves forward.' },
  ]

  // One panel per archetype, in the order the brand states them: the explorer
  // walks first, the hawk sees the whole territory, and the boundary is what
  // the two of them agree on.
  const TRAIL = [
    { id: 'fire',   art: '/brand/trail-fire.jpg',   title: 'A loyal companion',
      line: 'One schema, and every realm derives from it. Nothing to keep in step by hand.',
      alt:  'An explorer and a wolf at a campfire under a canyon wall at dusk' },
    { id: 'stake',  art: '/brand/trail-stake.jpg',  title: 'Guided by Litestone',
      line: 'Gates, policies and constraints are declared at the data boundary, not remembered in a hook.',
      alt:  'A hawk on a rock above a canyon, watching a glowing blue stake on the floor below' },
    { id: 'bounds', art: '/brand/trail-bounds.jpg', title: 'Boundaries create clarity',
      line: 'Clear limits make a system you can still reason about in a year. Predictable beats clever.',
      alt:  'An explorer at a row of carved stones lit with blue marks across a desert floor' },
  ]
</script>

<!--
  Full bleed. `.site-main` in _module.mesa pads its block edges by --space-4xl,
  so the hero escapes it with the SAME rung negated — a literal here would stay
  put the day that padding moves, and the seam under the topbar would reopen.
-->
<section class="hero theme-dark">
  <div class="container hero-inner">
    <div class="hero-copy stack">
      <p class="hero-kicker">FrontierJS · Built for The Wild</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title">Let’s get our hands dirty</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">
        FrontierJS is the JavaScript framework for the long road ahead. Built for
        makers, explorers and problem solvers who venture beyond the easy path.
        Out here, we build things that last.
      </p>
      <div class="cluster hero-cta">
        <!--
          `--bg-mix` is how a chip takes a hue the theme does not declare, and
          chip.css derives the label color from that fill's own luminance — so
          Mesa Orange gets dark text rather than the white that would sit on it
          at 2.97:1. Setting `color` here by hand is what breaks that.
        -->
        <a class="btn hero-btn" href="#merch">Get the merch</a>
        <a class="btn outlined hero-btn-2" href="/catalog/">Browse the catalogue</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!--
      The overlook, at its own aspect rather than as the band's ground. The
      plate is 1.34:1 and the band is nearly 2.8:1, so `cover` would crop half
      its height away whatever the position and the wolf would leave the frame.
      Framed, the whole animal is in shot, the hawk is still in the sky behind
      him, and at 2× it is sharp.
    -->
    <figure class="hero-art">
      <img src="/brand/hero.jpg" alt="A wolf standing on a canyon overlook at sunset, a hawk circling behind him" width="968" height="720" />
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="creed theme-dark">
  <div class="container creed-inner">
    <p class="creed-line">We explore. We build. We ship what matters.</p>
    <p class="creed-line creed-line-soft">No shortcuts. No fluff. Just solid tools for real developers.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="container stack band">
  <section class="stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">The three archetypes</p>
    <h2>One source of truth</h2>
    <ul class="archetypes">
      {#each ARCHETYPES as a}
        <li class="archetype stack">
          <!--
            The disc is decorative: the name is the <h3> right beside it, so an
            alt describing the same thing is read twice. Empty alt, not a
            missing one.
          -->
          <img class="archetype-art" src={a.art} alt="" width="120" height="120" loading="lazy" />
          <h3 class="h5">{a.name}</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">{a.line}</p>
        </li>
      {/each}
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>

<!-- ── Get the merch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `#home-featured` and one `[data-slug]` per product are what `verify:site`
     counts to prove the grid was baked rather than fetched. Renaming either is
     a drive edit, not a free rename. -->
<section class="merch" id="merch">
  <div class="container stack">
    <div class="merch-head">
      <div class="stack merch-head-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Supply Co.</p>
        <h2 class="merch-title">Get the merch</h2>
        <p class="text-muted">
          Tees, hoodies, caps and mugs for the three realms. Printed on the good
          stuff, shipped from the frontier.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn outlined" href="/catalog/">See everything</a>
    </div>

    <ul class="cards" id="home-featured">
      {#each products as p}
        <li class="card merch-card" data-slug={p.slug}>
          <a class="card-link" href={`/products/${p.slug}/`}>
            {#if p.image}
              <img class="card-shot" src={p.image.src} alt={p.image.alt} loading="lazy" />
            {/if}
            <strong>{p.name}</strong>
            <span class="price">{priceRange(p.from, p.to)}</span>
            {#if p.blurb}<p class="text-sm text-muted">{p.blurb}</p>{/if}
          </a>
        </li>
      {:else}
        <!--
          True in both places and that is the point of writing it here rather
          than something about the build: in the published page it means the
          shop has nothing active, and under `vite dev` it means `data` is null
          because this route's `load()` runs at build time. Sierra says the
          second one in the console; the page says what it can see.
        -->
        <li class="text-muted text-sm">Nothing to show.</li>
      {/each}
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="trail theme-dark">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why we build this way</p>
    <ul class="trail-list">
      {#each TRAIL as t}
        <li class="trail-item">
          <img class="trail-art" src={t.art} alt={t.alt} loading="lazy" />
          <h3 class="h5">{t.title}</h3>
          <p class="text-sm">{t.line}</p>
        </li>
      {/each}
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="container stack band">
  <p class="closing">
    Every page on this storefront was written ahead of time and the parts that
    have to be current — stock, your basket, the price on the shelf — ask the
    shop when you open it.
  </p>
  <p><a class="btn" href="/catalog/">Browse the whole catalogue</a></p>
</div>

<style>
  /* ── Shared ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  /*
   * The small caps that name a section. --label-* is the package's own triple
   * for this role, shared with a table head and a nav group label, so a theme
   * that restyles those restyles these.
   */
  .eyebrow {
    margin:         0;
    font-size:      var(--text-2xs);
    font-weight:    var(--label-font-weight);
    text-transform: var(--label-text-transform);
    letter-spacing: var(--label-letter-spacing);
    color:          var(--ink-mute);
  }

  .band {
    padding-block: var(--space-5xl);
  }

  /* ── Hero ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .hero {
    /*
     * The brand's Dusk Navy, over `theme-dark`'s ramp. Only the grounds are
     * restated: the inks, the rules and the tone-as-text window come from the
     * theme class, which is what keeps a toned chip in here legible without
     * this file knowing the rule.
     */
    --surface:        #0b182b;
    --surface-raised: #14263e;
    --surface-sunken: #060f1c;

    position: relative;
    /* The same rung `.site-main` pads with, negated — see the note above. */
    margin-block-start: calc(var(--space-4xl) * -1);
    /*
     * The ground carries atmosphere and no subject, and it is cut to be that:
     * the canyon to the RIGHT of the wolf on the same plate the hero art comes
     * from, so it shares the painting's light without repeating what is in the
     * frame beside it. With the wolf in both, his blurred silhouette sat
     * directly above the headline and read as a ghost of the plate — and a
     * scrim cannot settle that, because the animal is dark against a bright
     * sky and darkening the band darkens both.
     *
     * It ships DEFOCUSED — a 10px blur baked into the file rather than a CSS
     * filter, which would need a second stacking layer to avoid blurring the
     * copy over it — and upscaled before the blur, since the crop is narrower
     * than the band and `cover` would otherwise sharpen it back up.
     */
    background:
      var(--surface)
      url('/brand/ridge.jpg') center / cover no-repeat;
    color: var(--ink);
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /*
   * The scrim. Heavy and near-even, because the ground is carrying atmosphere
   * rather than a subject — and both halves of the band now hold something that
   * has to read: the copy on the left, the overlook plate on the right.
   */
  .hero::before {
    content:  '';
    position: absolute;
    inset:    0;
    z-index:  -1;

    background:
    linear-gradient( 100deg, rgba(6, 15, 28, 0.95) 0%, rgba(6, 15, 28, 0.55) 46%, rgba(6, 15, 28, 0.74) 100% )
  }

  .hero-inner {
    display:       grid;
    gap:           var(--space-5xl);
    align-items:   center;
    min-height:    clamp(24rem, 44vw, 33rem);
    padding-block: var(--space-5xl);
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .hero-inner {
      /* The copy leads; the plate is a shade narrower so the headline keeps the
         measure it was set for. */
      grid-template-columns: 1.05fr 0.95fr;
    }
  }

  .hero-copy {
    max-width: 38rem;
    gap:       var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .hero-art {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .hero-art img {
    display:       block;
    inline-size:   100%;
    block-size:    auto;
    border-radius: var(--card-radius);
    /* A warm hairline rather than --rule: the plate's own edge is sunset and a
       neutral gray line reads as a seam against it. */
    border:     var(--border-width) solid rgba(216, 184, 146, 0.35);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-lg);
  }

  .hero-kicker {
    margin:         0;
    font-size:      var(--text-xs);
    font-weight:    var(--label-font-weight);
    text-transform: var(--label-text-transform);
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    color:          #d8b892;
  }

  .hero-title {
    margin:      0;
    /* One step above the ladder's top rung: this is the only display type on
       the surface, and --text-4xl is tuned for a page title in a console. */
    font-size:   clamp(var(--text-4xl), 6vw, 3.75rem);
    line-height: var(--leading-display);
    text-wrap:   balance;
  }

  .hero-lede {
    margin:      0;
    max-width:   34rem;
    font-size:   var(--text-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    color:       var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .hero-cta {
    gap: var(--space-lg);
  }

  /* Mesa Orange, as a fill the chip lineage derives its own label color from. */
  .hero-btn {
    --bg-mix: #e07b39;
  }
  /*
   * Sandstone, and `.outlined` renders a tone as TEXT — so this reads through
   * tones.css's legibility window rather than being painted raw, and the window
   * is the inverted one because `theme-dark` is on the section.
   */
  .hero-btn-2 {
    --bg-mix: #d8b892;
  }

  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    /* One column: the plate stacks under the copy, and at that width it is the
       first thing scrolled past rather than a companion to the headline. */
    .hero-art {
      max-inline-size: 26rem;
    }
  }

  /* ── Creed ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .creed {
    --surface: #060f1c;
    background:    var(--surface);
    color:         var(--ink);
    border-block-end: var(--border-width) solid var(--rule);
  }
  .creed-inner {
    display:        flex;
    flex-wrap:      wrap;
    gap:            var(--space-sm) var(--space-4xl);
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding-block:  var(--space-2xl);
  }
  .creed-line {
    margin:         0;
    font-size:      var(--text-sm);
    font-weight:    var(--label-font-weight);
    text-transform: var(--label-text-transform);
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color:          #d8b892;
  }
  .creed-line-soft {
    color: var(--ink-mute);
  }

  /* ── Archetypes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .archetypes {
    list-style: none;
    padding:    0;
    margin:     0;
    display:    grid;
    gap:        var(--space-4xl);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr));
  }
  .archetype {
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .archetype-art {
    inline-size: 7.5rem;
    block-size:  7.5rem;
    /* The disc is already round with a transparent surround; the ring is the
       artwork's own. */
    border-radius: var(--pill-radius);
  }

  /* ── Merch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .merch {
    background:    var(--surface-sunken);
    border-block:  var(--border-width) solid var(--rule);
    padding-block: var(--space-5xl);
    /* An anchor target under a sticky header would otherwise land its own
       heading behind the topbar. The topbar here is not sticky, but the rung
       is what a reader expects to see above a jumped-to section either way. */
    scroll-margin-block-start: var(--space-4xl);
  }
  .merch-head {
    display:         flex;
    flex-wrap:       wrap;
    align-items:     end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap:             var(--space-2xl);
  }
  .merch-head-copy {
    gap:       var(--space-sm);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }
  .merch-title {
    margin:         0;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }
  .merch-card {
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  /* ── Trail ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .trail {
    --surface:        #0b182b;
    --surface-raised: #14263e;
    --surface-sunken: #060f1c;
    background:    var(--surface);
    color:         var(--ink);
    padding-block: var(--space-5xl);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-5xl);
  }
  .trail-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding:    0;
    margin:     0;
    display:    grid;
    gap:        var(--space-4xl);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr));
  }
  .trail-item > * + * {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .trail-art {
    inline-size: 100%;
    /*
     * 4/3, which is what the plates are cut at. At 1/1 `cover` took a square
     * out of the middle of each and the hawk — which sits at the left edge of
     * its plate — lost a wing to it.
     */
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    object-fit:   cover;
    border-radius: var(--card-radius);
    /* The frame is what stops a dark painting bleeding into a dark section. */
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--rule-strong);
  }
  .trail-item p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  /* ── Cards ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  .cards {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2xl);
    /* `minmax(0, …)` and not `auto`: an <img> at width:100% contributes no
       intrinsic minimum, and a track sized from content collapses the card.

       22rem rather than 16: the grid holds six, and at the container's own
       content width a 16rem track fits four — which orphans two on a second
       row. Three across is two clean rows. */
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr));
  }
  .card-link {
    display: grid;
    /* start, not stretch: a Badge is an inline term, and a grid item defaults
       to filling its column — which turns a price into a full-width bar. */
    justify-items: start;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .card-shot {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    object-fit: contain;
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    border-radius: var(--card-radius);
  }

  /*
   * A price is the second thing a shopper reads and it was a muted chip — the
   * Badge term is a categorical STATUS, so a price in one is both the wrong
   * vocabulary and the wrong emphasis.
   */
  .price {
    font-size:   var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 600;
    color:       var(--ink);
  }

  .closing {
    margin:    0;
    max-width: 42rem;
    color:     var(--ink-soft);
  }
</style>
